ility later.\nI\'m going with (E, 3).\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: The move (E, 3) is the strongest strategic option available. By playing (E, 3), you flip the piece at E4, resulting in a solid horizontal line of your pieces at E3, E4, and E5, directly parallel to the opponent\'s line at D3, D4, and D5. This formation creates a "wall" that minimizes the opponent\'s immediate mobility. Specifically, it restricts the opponent\'s valid moves primarily to the F-column (F2 through F6), forcing them to play to the outside of the group. This allows you to maintain control of the center and dictate the direction of the game, whereas other moves like (C, 5) would allow the opponent to expand in multiple directions (South and West), and (C, 3) would allow the opponent to immediately recapture the center with C4.\n\nReason: It maximizes the restriction of the opponent\'s mobility by limiting their responses to a single side of the board (the East/F-column), while maintaining a compact and connected central group of pieces.\n\nAnswer: (E, 3)'}
