nent. Bad.\n\nAfter deep analysis, it seems (D, 2) makes the best sense, creating a controlled, horizontal shape. I think it makes the most sense. The horizontal shape will limit opponent\'s movements. I can win this!\n\n\n', 'output': "Evaluation: Move (D, 2) flips two opponent pieces at D3 and D4, creating a solid horizontal line of four friendly pieces from D2 to D5. This move consolidates control in the center-left area of the board without immediately exposing the new piece to capture. In contrast, move (F, 5) would place a piece at F5 adjacent to the opponent's piece at F6, allowing the opponent to immediately play at F4 and flip F5 back, making it a tactical error. Moves (F, 2) and (G, 2) extend the frontier unnecessarily into empty space, giving the opponent more surface area to attack.\n\nReason: (D, 2) is the best move because it strengthens my position by creating a connected block of pieces and does not immediately give the opponent an easy tactical response, whereas (F, 5) is unstable and the other moves expand the frontier too aggressively.\n\nAnswer: (D, 2)"}
